A statement that a skeleton adds to an inherited method body may no longer call a method whose `modifies` clause is not empty (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/412). What the inherited body establishes, such as its postconditions, is assumed in the refining module rather than proved again, on the ground that added statements cannot change state the parent module knows about. Assignments to such state were already rejected, but a call was not: `...; Set2(c);` could set `c.f` to 2 after an inherited body that ensures `c.f == 1`, so the refined method had both postconditions and a caller could prove `false`. Such a call is now a resolution error, wherever it appears among the added statements, including a call to a ghost method or to a method declared in the refining module. Calls to lemmas, to methods with no `modifies` clause, and the initializing call of `new` (checked as before) are still allowed. No program in the test suite or the standard library is affected. The hidden option `--legacy-skeleton-modifying-calls` restores the old, unsound rule, for migration and comparison only.
